1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,680
In 1882, the zoologist Élie Metchnikoff pushed a

2
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:06,507
rose thorn into a see-through starfish larva.

3
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:10,000
By the next morning, moving cells had crowded

4
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:10,880
around the thorn.

5
00:00:10,987 --> 00:00:14,800
A starfish larva makes no antibodies, yet its

6
00:00:14,920 --> 00:00:17,680
cells found the intruder and closed in.

7
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:20,240
Your body runs a similar response when a

8
00:00:20,320 --> 00:00:22,880
splinter carries bacteria into your skin.

9
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:26,160
So what happens first, before any defence built

10
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:27,680
for that pathogen is ready?

11
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:31,860
In the last video, a banksia had to

12
00:00:31,940 --> 00:00:33,940
defend each cell where it stood.

13
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:37,100
Animals can do something plants can't.

14
00:00:37,380 --> 00:00:39,860
They carry defending cells in the blood and

15
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:42,340
send them to where a pathogen gets in.

16
00:00:42,540 --> 00:00:44,340
First, it has to get in.

17
00:00:46,367 --> 00:00:49,086
Most microbes never get past the outside.

18
00:00:49,487 --> 00:00:51,566
Your skin is the main wall.

19
00:00:51,807 --> 00:00:54,206
Its outer layers are tough, dead cells that

20
00:00:54,287 --> 00:00:57,326
keep flaking away, taking microbes with them.

21
00:00:57,647 --> 00:01:00,046
The airways are lined with sticky mucus that

22
00:01:00,127 --> 00:01:03,806
traps microbes, and tiny hair-like cilia beat and

23
00:01:03,967 --> 00:01:05,326
sweep it up to the throat to be

24
00:01:05,407 --> 00:01:06,206
swallowed.

25
00:01:06,527 --> 00:01:07,926
Chemicals help too.

26
00:01:08,207 --> 00:01:12,206
Stomach acid kills many swallowed microbes, and lysozyme,

27
00:01:12,247 --> 00:01:14,926
an enzyme in tears and saliva, breaks down

28
00:01:15,007 --> 00:01:16,766
the walls of many bacteria.

29
00:01:18,767 --> 00:01:21,406
A splinter breaks straight through that wall and

30
00:01:21,487 --> 00:01:24,846
pushes bacteria into the living tissue underneath.

31
00:01:24,927 --> 00:01:27,086
Now the body needs defences that act at

32
00:01:27,167 --> 00:01:30,046
once, against almost any invader.

33
00:01:30,127 --> 00:01:32,446
With the barriers, these make up the innate

34
00:01:32,527 --> 00:01:35,966
immune system: defences you're born with, which respond

35
00:01:36,087 --> 00:01:37,966
in much the same way to many different

36
00:01:38,047 --> 00:01:38,846
pathogens.

37
00:01:40,867 --> 00:01:43,986
Damaged cells raise the alarm with chemical signals,

38
00:01:44,387 --> 00:01:46,746
and so do immune cells already waiting in

39
00:01:46,787 --> 00:01:47,666
the tissue.

40
00:01:47,987 --> 00:01:51,186
Mast cells, which sit beside small blood vessels,

41
00:01:51,267 --> 00:01:53,826
release a chemical called histamine.

42
00:01:53,947 --> 00:01:56,866
Histamine makes those vessels widen, so more blood

43
00:01:56,947 --> 00:01:58,066
flows in.

44
00:01:58,147 --> 00:02:00,786
It also makes their walls leaky, so fluid

45
00:02:00,867 --> 00:02:03,506
and blood proteins seep out into the tissue.

46
00:02:05,534 --> 00:02:08,493
This response is inflammation, and you can see

47
00:02:08,574 --> 00:02:09,133
it.

48
00:02:09,214 --> 00:02:11,533
The extra blood makes the area red and

49
00:02:11,734 --> 00:02:12,333
warm.

50
00:02:12,374 --> 00:02:14,733
The leaked fluid makes it swell.

51
00:02:14,854 --> 00:02:17,053
Chemicals made at the site, plus the pressure

52
00:02:17,094 --> 00:02:19,773
of the swelling, set off pain nerves, so

53
00:02:19,854 --> 00:02:21,053
it hurts.

54
00:02:21,174 --> 00:02:23,533
These signs aren't the body failing.

55
00:02:23,614 --> 00:02:26,893
The wider, leakier vessels deliver defending cells and

56
00:02:26,974 --> 00:02:29,373
helpful proteins right to the bacteria.

57
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:34,520
The tissue also releases signal chemicals that spread

58
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:37,320
out from the infection, so they're strongest near

59
00:02:37,360 --> 00:02:38,120
the bacteria.

60
00:02:38,760 --> 00:02:41,800
Neutrophils, white blood cells carried in the blood,

61
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:44,520
stick to the vessel wall near the infection,

62
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:46,920
squeeze out between the cells of its wall,

63
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:49,360
and crawl towards the stronger signal.

64
00:02:49,640 --> 00:02:51,800
They're usually the first cells to arrive in

65
00:02:51,960 --> 00:02:53,880
large numbers, within hours.

66
00:02:55,900 --> 00:02:59,660
Neutrophils, and larger cells called macrophages that live

67
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:03,340
in the tissue, are phagocytes: cells that engulf

68
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:04,580
and digest.

69
00:03:04,860 --> 00:03:07,900
Receptors on a phagocyte bind molecules found on

70
00:03:08,020 --> 00:03:11,260
many bacteria but not on our own cells.

71
00:03:11,340 --> 00:03:14,540
The phagocyte wraps its membrane around the bacterium

72
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:17,820
and pulls it inside, sealed in a bubble.

73
00:03:17,980 --> 00:03:21,900
Then lysosomes, sacs of digestive enzymes, join the

74
00:03:21,980 --> 00:03:25,100
bubble, and the enzymes break the bacterium down.

75
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:29,500
Neutrophils die after a short time.

76
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:33,100
Their remains, with dead bacteria and fluid, can

77
00:03:33,300 --> 00:03:35,820
collect as pus, a sign the response has

78
00:03:35,900 --> 00:03:36,860
been fighting there.

79
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:40,140
If an infection spreads, signals in the blood

80
00:03:40,260 --> 00:03:43,340
can also raise the body's temperature, a fever,

81
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:45,580
which can speed up some immune reactions.

82
00:03:46,220 --> 00:03:49,020
Often, the innate response clears a small infection

83
00:03:49,060 --> 00:03:49,900
on its own.

84
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:55,260
Meanwhile, the body starts building a second defence,

85
00:03:55,380 --> 00:03:57,380
aimed at this exact pathogen.

86
00:03:57,660 --> 00:04:01,820
Dendritic cells, phagocytes with long, branching arms, take

87
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:03,660
up bacteria in the tissue.

88
00:04:03,980 --> 00:04:06,140
Then they travel along lymph vessels to a

89
00:04:06,220 --> 00:04:08,780
lymph node, a small organ where white blood

90
00:04:08,860 --> 00:04:11,500
cells gather, and show pieces of the bacteria

91
00:04:11,580 --> 00:04:12,780
to T cells.

92
00:04:13,100 --> 00:04:16,140
That starts the adaptive response, which takes days

93
00:04:16,220 --> 00:04:16,860
to build.

94
00:04:18,867 --> 00:04:21,826
So the innate response is fast and broad.

95
00:04:22,227 --> 00:04:24,866
It starts within minutes and works against almost

96
00:04:25,027 --> 00:04:27,666
any invader, but it isn't aimed at one

97
00:04:27,747 --> 00:04:30,786
pathogen, and it doesn't get better with practice.

98
00:04:31,107 --> 00:04:33,986
The adaptive response is slow the first time,

99
00:04:34,147 --> 00:04:35,266
but it's specific.

100
00:04:35,667 --> 00:04:37,186
And the two work together.

101
00:04:37,427 --> 00:04:40,146
Innate cells hold the bacteria back, and they

102
00:04:40,267 --> 00:04:42,386
show the adaptive cells what to target.

103
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:48,800
So remember the order: block, alarm, eat, alert.

104
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:54,480
Barriers block, inflammation raises the alarm, phagocytes eat,

105
00:04:54,560 --> 00:04:57,520
and dendritic cells alert the adaptive response.

106
00:04:59,534 --> 00:05:01,213
Two exam questions.

107
00:05:01,534 --> 00:05:02,013
First.

108
00:05:02,654 --> 00:05:05,213
In an experiment, a blue dye that binds

109
00:05:05,254 --> 00:05:07,933
to blood proteins was injected into a mouse's

110
00:05:08,094 --> 00:05:08,333
blood.

111
00:05:08,974 --> 00:05:11,613
Histamine was then injected into one small patch

112
00:05:11,654 --> 00:05:14,413
of its skin, and salt solution into another

113
00:05:14,574 --> 00:05:15,733
as a control.

114
00:05:16,094 --> 00:05:18,893
Within fifteen minutes, the histamine patch had turned

115
00:05:18,974 --> 00:05:21,373
blue, but the control patch hadn't.

116
00:05:21,934 --> 00:05:24,613
Explain this result, and how this change helps

117
00:05:24,654 --> 00:05:26,893
defend tissue against bacteria.

118
00:05:27,214 --> 00:05:28,573
Pause here to answer.

119
00:05:39,800 --> 00:05:42,360
Histamine made the small vessels there leaky.

120
00:05:42,840 --> 00:05:45,480
Fluid escaped into the skin, carrying the blood

121
00:05:45,560 --> 00:05:47,960
proteins and the dye stuck to them, so

122
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:49,560
the skin turned blue.

123
00:05:49,880 --> 00:05:52,680
The control shows the injection alone didn't cause

124
00:05:52,760 --> 00:05:53,200
it.

125
00:05:53,480 --> 00:05:56,520
In an infection, leaky vessels let proteins and

126
00:05:56,600 --> 00:05:58,920
white blood cells reach the bacteria.

127
00:05:59,240 --> 00:06:01,800
Saying the vessels just widened is the classic

128
00:06:01,960 --> 00:06:04,920
mistake, because only leaky walls let the dye

129
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:05,400
out.

130
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:07,960
Second.

131
00:06:08,440 --> 00:06:11,000
A child has an inherited condition in which

132
00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:13,480
neutrophils can't stick to the walls of blood

133
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:14,120
vessels.

134
00:06:14,760 --> 00:06:17,880
Their blood contains more neutrophils than normal, yet

135
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:21,000
they get repeated bacterial skin infections, and the

136
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:23,720
infected sites form little or no pus.

137
00:06:24,280 --> 00:06:25,880
Explain these observations.

138
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:27,960
Pause here to answer.

139
00:06:39,167 --> 00:06:41,726
Neutrophils must stick to the vessel wall to

140
00:06:41,807 --> 00:06:43,246
squeeze out into tissue.

141
00:06:43,807 --> 00:06:46,206
These can't, so they stay in the blood,

142
00:06:46,287 --> 00:06:47,726
which is why the count is high.

143
00:06:48,367 --> 00:06:51,246
Few reach the bacteria, so fewer bacteria are

144
00:06:51,367 --> 00:06:54,046
engulfed, and infections keep coming back.

145
00:06:54,607 --> 00:06:57,806
Pus is mostly dead neutrophils, so little pus

146
00:06:58,087 --> 00:06:58,846
forms.

147
00:06:59,327 --> 00:07:01,326
Thinking more neutrophils in the blood means a

148
00:07:01,487 --> 00:07:03,966
stronger defence is the classic mistake.

149
00:07:05,967 --> 00:07:09,646
So for any infection question, follow the order:

150
00:07:09,727 --> 00:07:12,926
which barrier was crossed, how inflammation changes the

151
00:07:13,007 --> 00:07:16,926
vessels, how phagocytes reach and digest the pathogen,

152
00:07:17,007 --> 00:07:19,086
and how adaptive cells are alerted.

